Harden the vepu5xx encoder slice poll loops against poll failure.

Conformance-tree copy of the MPP userspace half of the RKVENC2 slice-FIFO fix,
rebased onto the MANIFEST commit c2c1ee502b3a. The packaging copy, split into
two reviewable patches against the 1375813c packaging baseline, is authoritative:

  packaging/ppa/mpp/debian/patches/0002-harden-encoder-slice-poll-loops.patch
  packaging/ppa/mpp/debian/patches/0003-fix-h264e-poll-cfg-allocation-size.patch

Squashed here because bootstrap-sources.sh commits an applied patch set as a
single commit anyway, and because the two bases differ so the packaging patches
do not apply to this tree unchanged.

What it does:

  - the eight split-output slice poll loops assign the MPP_DEV_CMD_POLL return
    value but never test it, and terminate only on a last flag read out of the
    returned records; the four h264e HALs also leave that flag uninitialised, so
    an error with count_ret == 0 spins forever;
  - each loop now consumes the returned records first, then fails, and bounds
    consecutive empty-but-successful polls;
  - the terminal ENC_OUTPUT_FINISH callback is emitted when a loop is abandoned
    mid-frame, gated on a per-frame flag rather than on ctx->output_cb->cmd,
    which persists across frames;
  - the h264e poll cfg allocation is sized from the struct rather than the
    pointer, and hal_h264e_vepu511a.c stops indexing its single-cfg allocation.

Without this, the conformance MPP binaries hang instead of terminating when the
kernel reports a poll error, so the split_arg=4 reproducer cannot be scored.

See findings/2026-07-20-rkvenc2-slice-fifo-terminal-drop.md.

